  "slug": "heat-stress-95b0",
  "summary": "Recent preprints and a proteomics study show plant heat responses depend on stress combinations, priming and circadian timing.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across these items is how plants integrate heat with other stresses through chromatin, hormone and clock regulation. In potato, combined heat and drought alter chromatin accessibility and prime ABA biosynthesis via StNCED3 on repeated exposure. In rice, thermopriming produces a restrained, lower-entropy heat transcriptional response with H3K27me3 retention and memory chaperone expression. In Arabidopsis, combined heat and phosphate starvation yields organ-specific jasmonate changes, with JA-Ile increasing in roots and JA decreasing in shoots. Clock-focused Arabidopsis work links CCA1 and LHY to time-of-day heat responses across transcriptomic, proteomic and metabolomic layers, while a proteomics study of rve4 6 8 mutants connects clock genes to osmotic and salt stress. The new emphasis is on priming and organ-level divergence under stress combinations; the clock and salt-stress studies address related but distinct contexts.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-25"
}
